.\" vmxctl.1 — manual for vmxplore's command-line jobs. mdoc, OpenBSD section
.\" order. Keep in lockstep with the usage text in main.go (usageTmpl).
.Dd October 1, 2026
.Dt VMXCTL 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm vmxctl
.Nd vmxplore's jobs from the command line: appliances, reconcile, sysdiag
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -once
.Op Fl -reconcile Op Fl -orphans
.Op Fl -connect Ar dest
.Op Fl -rules Ar file
.Nm
.Fl -appliances
.Nm
.Fl -appliance Ar name Fl -vm Ar vmname
.Op Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value ...
.Nm
.Fl -build-all
.Op Fl -only Ar a,b
.Nm
.Fl -destroy-all
.Op Fl -yes
.Nm
.Fl -selftest
.Op Fl -only Ar name
.Op Fl -keep
.Nm
.Fl -sysdiag
.Nm
.Fl -setup
.Nm
.Fl -version
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
runs vmxplore's non-interactive jobs: building, listing and removing the
appliance catalog, reconciling the estate register against libvirt, and
diagnosing the host.
It is the same program as
.Xr vmxplore 1
built without the window, so it runs anywhere: over ssh, from a unit, in a
script.
The interactive consoles are
.Xr vmx 1
in a terminal and
.Xr vmxplore 1
in a window.
With no options,
.Nm
prints its usage and exits 2.
.Pp
.Nm vmxctl --help
is the complete option list, with every appliance option; this page covers
what each job does.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -once
Print the estate table once and exit.
.It Fl -reconcile Op Fl -orphans
Clear the unreconciled group: forget register rows for VMs libvirt no longer
has.
Orphan zvols are listed and kept; with
.Fl -orphans
they are destroyed too, snapshots included.
.It Fl -connect Ar dest
Drive a remote hypervisor: a host, user@host, or a libvirt URI such as
qemu+ssh://host/system.
.It Fl -rules Ar file
The grouping and classification rules
.Pq default Pa /etc/vmxplore/rules , else the built-in profile .
.It Fl -appliances
List the appliance catalog with each entry's settings.
.It Fl -appliance Ar name Fl -vm Ar vmname Op Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value ...
Build one appliance as a VM and wait until its service answers.
.It Fl -build-all Op Fl -only Ar a,b
Build one of every catalog appliance (or the listed ones), kept and shut off;
appliances that already exist are skipped.
.It Fl -destroy-all Op Fl -yes
Remove every VM the catalog built, and nothing else.
.It Fl -selftest Op Fl -only Ar name Op Fl -keep
Build and audit each appliance, then remove it unless
.Fl -keep .
.It Fl -sysdiag
Report what this host offers: the substrate, the probes, and what each
appliance can do here.
.It Fl -setup
Install the KVM stack on this machine, printing every command before it
runs.
.It Fl -version
Print the version and exit.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
Usage errors, including no options at all, exit 2.
.Sh EXAMPLES
List the catalog, then build Plex as a VM named plex:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ vmxctl --appliances
$ vmxctl --appliance "Plex on ZFS" --vm plex
.Ed
.Pp
Build every appliance, one at a time, and keep them shut off:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ vmxctl --build-all
.Ed
.Pp
Forget register rows for VMs that no longer exist, keeping orphan disks:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ vmxctl --reconcile
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr vmx 1 ,
.Xr vmxplore 1 ,
.Xr kfire 8
.Sh HISTORY
The jobs were options of the
.Nm vmx
binary until 30 September 2026, when
.Nm vmx
became the terminal console and the jobs moved to
.Nm .
The old spellings still work through
.Nm vmx ,
which hands them here with a warning.
